---
/*
  A ROUGH CUT, TO TEST A RHYTHM.

  Chris is considering building a scrolling piece out of one collage -
  cutting regions out of the original in Photoshop and letting them carry
  sections of a page. The expensive parts of that are rescanning, cutting,
  and inventing a section type. The cheap part is finding out whether moving
  from the whole piece to a fragment and back actually reads, which is the
  only thing that decides whether the rest is worth doing.

  So this is deliberately provisional:

    - it crops the 2000px web copy rather than the 6000px+ scan, because the
      question is about sequence, not sharpness. The fragments WILL be soft.
      Chris knows; that is the trade being made on purpose.
    - the rectangles are eyeballed from the composition, not authored in
      Studio. A real version would use the crop tool per section, or real
      cut-outs from the original document.
    - it is one hard-coded page rather than a section type, so nothing in
      the Page Builder or the dataset changes and deleting this file undoes
      all of it.
    - noindex, because a soft rough cut should not be the thing Google finds.

  WHY THESE FOUR REGIONS

  The piece is unusually text-dense - "Chasm of Aesthetic Reliability",
  "between liminal transit", the book paragraph about a healthy mind in an
  unsound body - and none of that is legible at tile size or even full-bleed
  at 2000px. So roughly a third of the work is invisible on the site today.
  That makes the cut lines fall on TEXT CLUSTERS rather than on visual
  quadrants: each region is one thought you can actually read.

  The captions are the artwork's own words. Nothing here is written about
  the piece, which would be a different and much worse page.
*/
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import {sanityClient} from '../../lib/sanity';
import {imageUrl, tileImage} from '../../lib/image';

/*
  PREVIEW ONLY, AND NOT BY POLITENESS.

  A rough cut with eyeballed crops should not be on rumeaudesign.co, and
  `noindex` plus "nothing links to it" is not the same as not being there -
  the URL would still answer, and anyone with the link would see provisional
  work on the real site.

  Returning an empty array emits NO page at all, so production builds the
  route out of existence rather than shipping a hidden one. That is the
  difference between a page that is hard to find and a page that does not
  exist, and only the second is true here.

  PUBLIC_IS_PREVIEW already distinguishes the two builds - true on the
  preview deploy, unset on production - so this needs no new flag and
  cannot drift from the thing it is meant to track.
*/
export function getStaticPaths() {
  if (import.meta.env.PUBLIC_IS_PREVIEW !== 'true') return [];
  return [{params: {piece: 'red-kettle'}}];
}

const piece = await sanityClient.fetch(
  `*[_type == "caseStudy" && slug.current == "collage-red-kettle-2025"][0]{
     title, thumbnail, mainImage
   }`,
);

const art = tileImage(piece);

/*
  Source rectangles in the 2000x2004 original, as [left, top, width, height].

  Eyeballed, and they will be a few percent out. That is tolerable for a
  question about rhythm and intolerable for anything that ships, which is
  part of why this page is not the Page Builder.
*/
const REGIONS = [
  {
    rect: [170, 50, 760, 1230] as const,
    line: 'How did it come to this?',
    note: 'Buddha Sought The Bluebird',
  },
  {
    rect: [830, 60, 1150, 650] as const,
    line: 'but then it returns…',
    note: 'between liminal transit',
  },
  {
    rect: [540, 620, 1440, 560] as const,
    line: 'neither above nor below',
    note: 'Red Kettle',
  },
  {
    rect: [100, 1180, 1800, 810] as const,
    line: 'joy as an act of resistance',
    note: 'storm on the rain',
  },
];

const whole = art ? imageUrl(art)?.width(1800).url() : null;
const crops = art
  ? REGIONS.map((r) => ({
      ...r,
      src: imageUrl(art)
        ?.rect(r.rect[0], r.rect[1], r.rect[2], r.rect[3])
        .width(1800)
        .url(),
    }))
  : [];
---

<Layout
  title="Red Kettle"
  description="A collage read in sequence - the whole piece, then the fragments it is made of."
  noindex
>
  {!art ? (
    <p>
      Could not find <code>collage-red-kettle-2025</code>, or it has no image attached.
    </p>
  ) : (
    <article class="plate">
      <header class="plate__open">
        <p class="plate__eyebrow">Collage</p>
        <h1 class="plate__title">Red Kettle</h1>
        <p class="plate__sub">Norfolk island pine &middot; 2026</p>
      </header>

      <figure class="plate__whole">
        <img src={whole} alt="Red Kettle, a square mixed-media collage on kraft cardboard" />
      </figure>

      {crops.map((c, i) => (
        /*
          The flip is a class, not :nth-child. The regions are not the only
          children of .plate - the header, the opening plate and the closing
          one are in there too - so nth-child would count those and alternate
          off the wrong parity. It happens to land correctly today, which is
          the worst kind of correct.
        */
        <section class:list={['plate__region', i % 2 === 1 && 'plate__region--flip']}>
          <figure class="plate__fig">
            <img src={c.src} alt={`Detail from Red Kettle: ${c.note}`} loading="lazy" />
          </figure>
          <div class="plate__words">
            <p class="plate__line">{c.line}</p>
            <p class="plate__note">{c.note}</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      ))}

      <figure class="plate__whole plate__whole--close">
        <img src={whole} alt="Red Kettle, the complete collage" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>

      <footer class="plate__close">
        <p class="plate__line plate__line--big">who is waiting on the other line?</p>
      </footer>
    </article>
  )}
</Layout>

<style>
  /*
    The kraft ground, taken from the cardboard the pieces are made on.

    This is the cheapest "design the site around the work" move available:
    the site's field becomes the collage's field, so a fragment sits on the
    page as though it were pasted there rather than floating in a gallery.
    Scoped to this page while it is a prototype - if it earns its place it
    belongs in global.css as a token.
  */
  .plate {
    --kraft: #b4946f;
    --kraft-deep: #9c7c57;
    --ink: #1a1613;
    --flag: #e24a24;

    background: var(--kraft);
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0 calc(50% - 50vw);
    padding: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 5rem) clamp(1rem, 4vw, 3rem);
  }

  .plate__open,
  .plate__close {
    max-width: 48rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .plate__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .plate__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 9vw, 5.5rem);
    line-height: 0.95;
    color: var(--flag);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .plate__sub {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .plate__whole {
    max-width: min(46rem, 100%);
    margin: clamp(2rem, 7vw, 5rem) auto;
  }

  .plate__whole img,
  .plate__fig img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /*
    One region per screenful, alternating which side the words sit on, so
    scrolling has a pulse rather than a march. The fragment is given the
    larger share because it is the thing being read.
  */
  .plate__region {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    align-items: center;
    max-width: 72rem;
    margin: 0 auto clamp(3rem, 10vw, 7rem);
  }

  .plate__region--flip {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 3fr);
  }

  .plate__region--flip .plate__fig {
    order: 2;
  }

  .plate__line {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.125rem, 2.2vw, 1.75rem);
    line-height: 1.25;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .plate__line--big {
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    color: var(--flag);
  }

  .plate__note {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    /* Dark enough to read as a margin annotation rather than as a second
       voice competing with the artwork's own line. A colour, not a filter -
       filter would darken the whole element and is a roundabout way of
       saying something CSS can say directly. */
    color: #5f4a33;
  }

  /* One column on a phone, words under the fragment, order never flipped -
     an alternating layout at this width just reads as inconsistent. */
  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .plate__region,
    .plate__region--flip {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 1rem;
    }

    .plate__region--flip .plate__fig {
      order: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
